⚙️ Prerequisites

Before running the project, ensure you have:

- An AWS account
- Terraform CLI installed
- AWS CLI installed and configured
- Git installed
- An S3 bucket configured for Terraform remote state
- An IAM role with the required permissions
- GitHub Actions OIDC configured in AWS IAM

🚀 Running Terraform Locally

1. Clone the repository

git clone https://github.com/dilip0509/terraform-aws-devops.git
cd terraform-aws-devops

2. Initialize Terraform

terraform init

3. Format the Terraform files

terraform fmt -recursive

4. Validate the configuration

terraform validate

5. Review the execution plan

terraform plan

6. Deploy infrastructure when ready

Review the plan and verify AWS permissions, service quotas, and estimated costs before running:

terraform apply

7. Destroy managed resources when finished

terraform destroy

Always review the destroy plan before confirming. Also check the AWS console for resources left behind by failed or partially completed deployments.

🔐 Security Best Practices

- Use IAM roles and OIDC for GitHub Actions authentication.
- Avoid storing AWS access keys in source code.
- Never commit Terraform state files, private keys, or secret variable files.
- Keep S3 Block Public Access enabled for the state bucket.
- Follow least-privilege IAM permissions.
- Review Terraform plans before applying changes.
- Use ".gitignore" to exclude local Terraform state and generated files.

💰 Cost Management

AWS resources may incur charges even when an application is not receiving traffic.

- Review costs for ALB, EC2, EBS, and other deployed resources.
- Use "terraform destroy" when the environment is no longer needed.
- Confirm that resources were successfully removed.
- Retain the S3 state bucket if you intend to continue using the project.

